      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Part 5 — Most important relationship from Day 2",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Part 5 — Most important relationship from Day 2",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "jLblLPTEz36yfj0helLJA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 427.5986653477696,
      "y": 20515.412077774046,
      "width": 748,
      "height": 1025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4c",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2105298145,
      "version": 38,
      "versionNonce": 2039055913,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Remember:\n\nUser\n   │\n   │ M:N\n   ▼\nOrganizationMember\n   │\n   ▼\nOrganization\n\nWe don't do:\nUser\n └── organization_id ❌\n\nbecause:\n\nAmit\n ├── Google\n ├── TechNova\n └── Personal Workspace\n\nInstead:\n\nUser\n  │\n  ├── OrganizationMember → Google\n  ├── OrganizationMember → TechNova\n  └── OrganizationMember → Personal\n\nAnd membership can contain:\n\nOrganizationMember\n-----------------------\nuser\norganization\nrole\nstatus\njoined_at\n\nThis is where our Day 2 database design directly becomes Django ORM.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Remember:\n\nUser\n   │\n   │ M:N\n   ▼\nOrganizationMember\n   │\n   ▼\nOrganization\n\nWe don't do:\nUser\n └── organization_id ❌\n\nbecause:\n\nAmit\n ├── Google\n ├── TechNova\n └── Personal Workspace\n\nInstead:\n\nUser\n  │\n  ├── OrganizationMember → Google\n  ├── OrganizationMember → TechNova\n  └── OrganizationMember → Personal\n\nAnd membership can contain:\n\nOrganizationMember\n-----------------------\nuser\norganization\nrole\nstatus\njoined_at\n\nThis is where our Day 2 database design directly becomes Django ORM.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "icy-WUzj6Jyd2JXDDMLYK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 419.5830403477696,
      "y": 21587.294890274046,
      "width": 385,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4e",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1497251983,
      "version": 33,
      "versionNonce": 1649937639,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🎯 Today's first exercise\n\nQuestion 1 : Suppose we have:\nUser\nOrganization\nOrganizationMember\n\nAnd Rahul belongs to:\nGoogle → Developer\nTechNova → Consultant\n\nWhere should we store Rahul's role?\n\nA) User.role\nB) Organization.role\nC) OrganizationMember.role\nD) Somewhere else",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🎯 Today's first exercise\n\nQuestion 1 : Suppose we have:\nUser\nOrganization\nOrganizationMember\n\nAnd Rahul belongs to:\nGoogle → Developer\nTechNova → Consultant\n\nWhere should we store Rahul's role?\n\nA) User.role\nB) Organization.role\nC) OrganizationMember.role\nD) Somewhere else",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "uLzBDDZlyCA3EK-I5IFcG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 453.4541340977696,
      "y": 22029.290984024046,
      "width": 836,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4f",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 745131311,
      "version": 54,
      "versionNonce": 413718793,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Correct. ✅\nYou chose C — OrganizationMember.role.\nYour reasoning is exactly right:\nA user's role can be different in different organizations.\n\nFor example:\nUser\nRahul\n\nMemberships:\nOrganizationMember\n--------------------------------\nRahul → Google    → Developer\nRahul → TechNova → Consultant\n\nSo role belongs to the relationship between Rahul and the organization, \nnot to Rahul himself.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong\nA) User.role ❌ -> Would mean Rahul has only one role:\nB) Organization.role ❌ ->  Would mean everyone in Google has the same role:\n\nThe general rule 🔑\n\nWhenever you see a statement like:\n\"X has a different value depending on Y\"\nthat value often belongs to the relationship/junction table.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Correct. ✅\nYou chose C — OrganizationMember.role.\nYour reasoning is exactly right:\nA user's role can be different in different organizations.\n\nFor example:\nUser\nRahul\n\nMemberships:\nOrganizationMember\n--------------------------------\nRahul → Google    → Developer\nRahul → TechNova → Consultant\n\nSo role belongs to the relationship between Rahul and the organization, \nnot to Rahul himself.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong\nA) User.role ❌ -> Would mean Rahul has only one role:\nB) Organization.role ❌ ->  Would mean everyone in Google has the same role:\n\nThe general rule 🔑\n\nWhenever you see a statement like:\n\"X has a different value depending on Y\"\nthat value often belongs to the relationship/junction table.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "II2KtyQgHEx66e5KZknv7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 369.4619465977696,
      "y": 22784.415984024046,
      "width": 1188,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4g",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 577943663,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1169414151,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 2: Why should we create a Custom User model in Django instead of using Django's default User model?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 2: Why should we create a Custom User model in Django instead of using Django's default User model?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "PfX52Lw4ShTWvvBLL3ZWw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 434.0439778477696,
      "y": 22824.685515274046,
      "width": 1012,
      "height": 1250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4h",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2067879233,
      "version": 52,
      "versionNonce": 35311593,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Django's default User model gives us common fields such as:\nusername\npassword\nemail\nfirst_name\nlast_name\n...\n\nBut our application may have requirements such as:\nUser\n├── email\n├── password\n├── first_name\n├── last_name\n├── phone_number\n├── email_verified\n├── profile_image\n├── created_at\n└── updated_at\n\nMore importantly, we may want email to be the login identity instead of username.\n\nFor example:\nPOST /login\n\nemail: amit@gmail.com\npassword: ********\n\nrather than:\nusername: amit123\npassword: ********\nWith a custom user model, we control these decisions from the beginning.\n\n\nWhy create it from Day 1?\nThis is important.\nChanging from Django's default User to a custom user model after your project already has \nmany migrations and relationships can be painful.\n\n\nWe use a custom User model when the application's authentication requirements differ from \nDjango's default User model. It gives us control over fields, authentication identity, and \nfuture extensibility. We should generally define it at the beginning of the project because \nchanging the user model later can be difficult due to existing relationships and migrations.\n\n\nYour core reasoning was correct. The missing points were:\nWe control the authentication identity (e.g., email instead of username).\nCustomization isn't only about adding fields.\nCreating it early avoids painful migration/relationship changes later.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Django's default User model gives us common fields such as:\nusername\npassword\nemail\nfirst_name\nlast_name\n...\n\nBut our application may have requirements such as:\nUser\n├── email\n├── password\n├── first_name\n├── last_name\n├── phone_number\n├── email_verified\n├── profile_image\n├── created_at\n└── updated_at\n\nMore importantly, we may want email to be the login identity instead of username.\n\nFor example:\nPOST /login\n\nemail: amit@gmail.com\npassword: ********\n\nrather than:\nusername: amit123\npassword: ********\nWith a custom user model, we control these decisions from the beginning.\n\n\nWhy create it from Day 1?\nThis is important.\nChanging from Django's default User to a custom user model after your project already has \nmany migrations and relationships can be painful.\n\n\nWe use a custom User model when the application's authentication requirements differ from \nDjango's default User model. It gives us control over fields, authentication identity, and \nfuture extensibility. We should generally define it at the beginning of the project because \nchanging the user model later can be difficult due to existing relationships and migrations.\n\n\nYour core reasoning was correct. The missing points were:\nWe control the authentication identity (e.g., email instead of username).\nCustomization isn't only about adding fields.\nCreating it early avoids painful migration/relationship changes later.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "L8ZAt_c04EsJgyq1NJ7Rx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 371.9619465977696,
      "y": 24143.915984024046,
      "width": 616,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4i",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1208621052,
      "version": 10,
      "versionNonce": 1571372839,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789700940217,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 3 Why should email be unique in our User model?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 3 Why should email be unique in our User model?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hhMxEjexZR3zvdRtf7uVb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 416.2119465977696,
      "y": 24178.990202774046,
      "width": 1045,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 677779524,
      "version": 27,
      "versionNonce": 468380361,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789700969598,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We make email unique because email acts as the user's unique authentication identity. This \nprevents duplicate accounts with the same email and ensures operations such as login, password \nreset, and email verification can reliably identify a single user. We also enforce this at the \ndatabase level to maintain data integrity.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We make email unique because email acts as the user's unique authentication identity. This \nprevents duplicate accounts with the same email and ensures operations such as login, password \nreset, and email verification can reliably identify a single user. We also enforce this at the \ndatabase level to maintain data integrity.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "L2N0LglFlcNjO5h-jwv_D",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 362.9580403477696,
      "y": 24380.025359024046,
      "width": 803,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4k",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 447121732,
      "version": 25,
      "versionNonce": 480559687,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789700999815,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 4 -> Now let's go one level deeper.\nSuppose Amit signs up with: Email: Amit@Gmail.com\nLater, he tries to log in with: Email: amit@gmail.com\nShould our system consider these the same email or different emails? Why?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 4 -> Now let's go one level deeper.\nSuppose Amit signs up with: Email: Amit@Gmail.com\nLater, he tries to log in with: Email: amit@gmail.com\nShould our system consider these the same email or different emails? Why?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "swmkGMrSovJG0Ber0Yqqv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 420.9619465977696,
      "y": 24503.158171524046,
      "width": 1221,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1698656580,
      "version": 35,
      "versionNonce": 2125769129,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789701219754,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "For normal email addresses, the domain part is case-insensitive, and in practice most \nauthentication systems treat the whole email address case-insensitively.\n\nOne important nuance\nStrictly speaking, the email standard technically permits the part before @ to be \ncase-sensitive. But almost all modern providers treat it case-insensitively.\nFor our application, we should therefore normalize emails consistently.\n\nemail = email.strip().lower()\nThen store: amit@gmail.com\n\nThis prevents:\nAmit@Gmail.com\namit@gmail.com\n\nfrom accidentally becoming two accounts.\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nWe should normalize email addresses before storing and comparing them, typically by trimming \nwhitespace and converting them to lowercase. This ensures that variations such as Amit@Gmail.com and \namit@gmail.com are treated as the same login identity. We should also enforce uniqueness at the database level.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "For normal email addresses, the domain part is case-insensitive, and in practice most \nauthentication systems treat the whole email address case-insensitively.\n\nOne important nuance\nStrictly speaking, the email standard technically permits the part before @ to be \ncase-sensitive. But almost all modern providers treat it case-insensitively.\nFor our application, we should therefore normalize emails consistently.\n\nemail = email.strip().lower()\nThen store: amit@gmail.com\n\nThis prevents:\nAmit@Gmail.com\namit@gmail.com\n\nfrom accidentally becoming two accounts.\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nWe should normalize email addresses before storing and comparing them, typically by trimming \nwhitespace and converting them to lowercase. This ensures that variations such as Amit@Gmail.com and \namit@gmail.com are treated as the same login identity. We should also enforce uniqueness at the database level.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "9RbOWoYQRdtUWsVH3sZfj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 339.9619465977696,
      "y": 25139.915984024046,
      "width": 418,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4n",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 390762876,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 1937957223,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789701336153,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 5 — Important Django question",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 5 — Important Django question",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "9qYz-EIqj7vwUoUVqZD4T",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 401.9619465977696,
      "y": 25174.915984024046,
      "width": 517,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4o",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1126192380,
      "version": 17,
      "versionNonce": 597920905,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789701503569,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We have:\nUser\nOrganization\nOrganizationMember\n\nAmit belongs to Google as an Owner.\nRahul belongs to Google as a Developer.\nNow imagine Amit leaves Google.\nWhat should happen to Amit's User account?\n\nShould we:\n\nA) Delete Amit's User\nB) Delete only Amit's OrganizationMember record\nC) Keep both User and OrganizationMember\nD) Something else?\n\nGive me your reasoning.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We have:\nUser\nOrganization\nOrganizationMember\n\nAmit belongs to Google as an Owner.\nRahul belongs to Google as a Developer.\nNow imagine Amit leaves Google.\nWhat should happen to Amit's User account?\n\nShould we:\n\nA) Delete Amit's User\nB) Delete only Amit's OrganizationMember record\nC) Keep both User and OrganizationMember\nD) Something else?\n\nGive me your reasoning.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vZWJjWbiHt5V5uMMm3y6W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 446.13084203487716,
      "y": 25654.11134975533,
      "width": 1144,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2049901436,
      "version": 27,
      "versionNonce": 59776135,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789701629701,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "B\nWe should not delete the User because the user represents a global identity that can belong to multiple \norganizations. When the user leaves one organization, we remove or deactivate their OrganizationMember \nrelationship for that organization.\n\nOne improvement ⚠️\nIn a production system, I wouldn't necessarily delete the membership row immediately.\n\nWe may have:\nOrganizationMember\n- status = ACTIVE\n- status = REMOVED\n\nWhy?\nBecause we may need an audit trail:\nAmit joined Google → Jan 2025\nAmit left Google  → Sep 2026\n\nThis becomes important for audit logs, compliance, historical reporting, etc.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "B\nWe should not delete the User because the user represents a global identity that can belong to multiple \norganizations. When the user leaves one organization, we remove or deactivate their OrganizationMember \nrelationship for that organization.\n\nOne improvement ⚠️\nIn a production system, I wouldn't necessarily delete the membership row immediately.\n\nWe may have:\nOrganizationMember\n- status = ACTIVE\n- status = REMOVED\n\nWhy?\nBecause we may need an audit trail:\nAmit joined Google → Jan 2025\nAmit left Google  → Sep 2026\n\nThis becomes important for audit logs, compliance, historical reporting, etc.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "NXzG5nAYPEp7EA8s7W9b6",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 370.87311007611413,
      "y": 26222.152586868728,
      "width": 759,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4s",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1988493252,
      "version": 24,
      "versionNonce": 1383273321,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789701719968,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 6\n\nNow let's make it slightly harder.\n\nAmit is:\nGoogle\n   ↓\nOrganizationMember\n   ↓\nOwner\n\nAmit also belongs to:\nEngineering Team\n   ↓\nTeamMember\n   ↓\nDeveloper\n\nNow Amit leaves the Engineering Team, but remains a member of Google.\n\nWhat should happen?\n\nA) Delete Amit's User\nB) Delete Amit's OrganizationMember\nC) Remove/deactivate Amit's TeamMember\nD) Delete the entire Team\n\nAnd most importantly:\nWhy?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 6\n\nNow let's make it slightly harder.\n\nAmit is:\nGoogle\n   ↓\nOrganizationMember\n   ↓\nOwner\n\nAmit also belongs to:\nEngineering Team\n   ↓\nTeamMember\n   ↓\nDeveloper\n\nNow Amit leaves the Engineering Team, but remains a member of Google.\n\nWhat should happen?\n\nA) Delete Amit's User\nB) Delete Amit's OrganizationMember\nC) Remove/deactivate Amit's TeamMember\nD) Delete the entire Team\n\nAnd most importantly:\nWhy?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "HynX3-nzUTlAJ7xbLvsFK",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 420.3267183235368,
      "y": 26959.265988930598,
      "width": 1078,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 318918908,
      "version": 11,
      "versionNonce": 277410727,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789701830102,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "C\nWhen a user leaves a team, we should remove or deactivate their TeamMember relationship rather \nthan deleting the user or organization membership. TeamMember represents the relationship between \na user and a team, so only that relationship should change. In a production system, we may \nsoft-delete or mark the membership as inactive to preserve audit history.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "C\nWhen a user leaves a team, we should remove or deactivate their TeamMember relationship rather \nthan deleting the user or organization membership. TeamMember represents the relationship between \na user and a team, so only that relationship should change. In a production system, we may \nsoft-delete or mark the membership as inactive to preserve audit history.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wOW2I847HhK2zr3R_n141",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 333.7597080142583,
      "y": 27187.101040477006,
      "width": 1155,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4u",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 451398468,
      "version": 21,
      "versionNonce": 1332657737,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789702068288,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 7 — Now an important database concept\nSuppose we have:\nOrganization\n    ↓\nTeam\n    ↓\nProject\n    ↓\nTask\n\nA Task belongs to a Project.\nA Project belongs to a Team.\nA Team belongs to an Organization.\n\nQuestion:\n\nIf Amit belongs to Google, but Rahul belongs to TechNova, and Amit tries to create a task inside Rahul's \nGoogle/TechNova project:\n\nWhere should we check whether Amit has permission to create that task?\nWould you check:\n\nA) Only in Task\nB) Only in User\nC) Through the Organization/Team/Project membership hierarchy\nD) Somewhere else?\n\nAnd explain why.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 7 — Now an important database concept\nSuppose we have:\nOrganization\n    ↓\nTeam\n    ↓\nProject\n    ↓\nTask\n\nA Task belongs to a Project.\nA Project belongs to a Team.\nA Team belongs to an Organization.\n\nQuestion:\n\nIf Amit belongs to Google, but Rahul belongs to TechNova, and Amit tries to create a task inside Rahul's \nGoogle/TechNova project:\n\nWhere should we check whether Amit has permission to create that task?\nWould you check:\n\nA) Only in Task\nB) Only in User\nC) Through the Organization/Team/Project membership hierarchy\nD) Somewhere else?\n\nAnd explain why.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "S6GXyZ04gOsGCLqFWIpJo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 395.6314863647736,
      "y": 27924.210415477013,
      "width": 1023,
      "height": 1325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4v",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1219869508,
      "version": 49,
      "versionNonce": 848946887,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789702126460,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "C) Through the Organization → Team → Project hierarchy is correct.\n\nThe important idea is that permission is not determined only by the Task or User. We need to \nestablish whether Amit has access to the organization/team/project that owns the task.\n\nBefore allowing: POST /projects/10/tasks/\n\nthe backend should verify something like:\n\n1. Is Amit a member of the organization?\n        ↓\n2. Is Amit a member of the relevant team?\n        ↓\n3. Does the project belong to that team?\n        ↓\n4. Does Amit's role allow task creation?\n        ↓\n5. Create the task\n\nAuthorization should be checked against the resource hierarchy. Since a task belongs to a \nproject, the project belongs to a team, and the team belongs to an organization, we need to \nverify that the authenticated user has access to that organization/team/project and has the \nrequired permission before creating or modifying the task.\n\nOne important correction\nDon't rely only on the hierarchy.\nWe also need role/permission checks.\nFor example:\n\nEngineering Team\n\nAmit → Admin\nRahul → Developer\nVikas → Viewer\n\nAll three may have access to the project, but their actions could differ:\n\nAdmin    → create/delete/manage\nDeveloper → create/update tasks\nViewer    → read only\n\nSo authorization is essentially:\nAuthentication\n      ↓\nWho is the user?\n      ↓\nResource ownership/access\n      ↓\nOrganization → Team → Project → Task\n      ↓\nRole / Permission\n      ↓\nAllow or Deny",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "C) Through the Organization → Team → Project hierarchy is correct.\n\nThe important idea is that permission is not determined only by the Task or User. We need to \nestablish whether Amit has access to the organization/team/project that owns the task.\n\nBefore allowing: POST /projects/10/tasks/\n\nthe backend should verify something like:\n\n1. Is Amit a member of the organization?\n        ↓\n2. Is Amit a member of the relevant team?\n        ↓\n3. Does the project belong to that team?\n        ↓\n4. Does Amit's role allow task creation?\n        ↓\n5. Create the task\n\nAuthorization should be checked against the resource hierarchy. Since a task belongs to a \nproject, the project belongs to a team, and the team belongs to an organization, we need to \nverify that the authenticated user has access to that organization/team/project and has the \nrequired permission before creating or modifying the task.\n\nOne important correction\nDon't rely only on the hierarchy.\nWe also need role/permission checks.\nFor example:\n\nEngineering Team\n\nAmit → Admin\nRahul → Developer\nVikas → Viewer\n\nAll three may have access to the project, but their actions could differ:\n\nAdmin    → create/delete/manage\nDeveloper → create/update tasks\nViewer    → read only\n\nSo authorization is essentially:\nAuthentication\n      ↓\nWho is the user?\n      ↓\nResource ownership/access\n      ↓\nOrganization → Team → Project → Task\n      ↓\nRole / Permission\n      ↓\nAllow or Deny",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "rYg2RQeJ-M4X-LKp2bDpT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 326.2854812101349,
      "y": 29361.074139703785,
      "width": 682,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4w",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 496099964,
      "version": 27,
      "versionNonce": 1232211241,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789702315308,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 8 — Very important Django concept\nSuppose Rahul is a member of Google.\n\nHe sends:\nPOST /api/projects/15/tasks/\nAuthorization: Bearer <Rahul's JWT>\n\nThe JWT tells us:\nuser_id = 42\n\nShould we trust a user_id sent in the request body like this?\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix login bug\",\n    \"user_id\": 99\n}\n\nOr should the backend get the user from the authenticated JWT?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 8 — Very important Django concept\nSuppose Rahul is a member of Google.\n\nHe sends:\nPOST /api/projects/15/tasks/\nAuthorization: Bearer <Rahul's JWT>\n\nThe JWT tells us:\nuser_id = 42\n\nShould we trust a user_id sent in the request body like this?\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix login bug\",\n    \"user_id\": 99\n}\n\nOr should the backend get the user from the authenticated JWT?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "5BsQXSEw5DvqFOVC3WJnJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 386.3370276018872,
      "y": 29809.906291765656,
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 400,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4y",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 327399748,
      "version": 13,
      "versionNonce": 1214215655,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789702453992,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The authenticated user should come from the authentication mechanism, typically request.user, \nwhich is established after verifying the JWT. We should not trust a client-provided user_id for \nidentity because the client can modify it. Request-body fields should be treated as untrusted input.\n\nOne nuance\n\nThe JWT itself isn't automatically trustworthy just because it exists.\n\nThe backend must first verify:\n\nSignature\nExpiration\nToken structure\nRelevant claims\n\nThen we can trust the authenticated identity represented by it.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The authenticated user should come from the authentication mechanism, typically request.user, \nwhich is established after verifying the JWT. We should not trust a client-provided user_id for \nidentity because the client can modify it. Request-body fields should be treated as untrusted input.\n\nOne nuance\n\nThe JWT itself isn't automatically trustworthy just because it exists.\n\nThe backend must first verify:\n\nSignature\nExpiration\nToken structure\nRelevant claims\n\nThen we can trust the authenticated identity represented by it.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "IzRkto-CRCBDaFHW0N6jN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 293.4619465977696,
      "y": 30307.544890274046,
      "width": 616,
      "height": 550,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b50",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1187407646,
      "version": 17,
      "versionNonce": 132677641,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789964137940,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 9 Our Task model is:\n\nTask\n- id\n- title\n- project_id\n- created_by\n- assigned_to\n- reviewed_by\n\nRahul creates a task.\n\nWho should populate created_by?\n\nA) Rahul sends created_by in the JSON request\nB) Backend gets the authenticated user from request.user\nC) Frontend sends Rahul's user ID in the URL\nD) Something else?\n\nAnd most importantly:\n\nWhy?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 9 Our Task model is:\n\nTask\n- id\n- title\n- project_id\n- created_by\n- assigned_to\n- reviewed_by\n\nRahul creates a task.\n\nWho should populate created_by?\n\nA) Rahul sends created_by in the JSON request\nB) Backend gets the authenticated user from request.user\nC) Frontend sends Rahul's user ID in the URL\nD) Something else?\n\nAnd most importantly:\n\nWhy?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "sqrqwE18VS2uZXADGQ5xo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 380.4619465977696,
      "y": 30892.904265274046,
      "width": 1067,
      "height": 1150,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b51",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1535531486,
      "version": 49,
      "versionNonce": 686170375,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789964179335,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "B) Backend gets the authenticated user from request.user.\n\nWhy?\ncreated_by represents who actually performed the action.\nThe client should not be allowed to decide that.\nFor example, Rahul sends:\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"Fix login bug\"\n}\n\nThe request contains Rahul's JWT:\n\nJWT\n ↓\nuser_id = 42\n ↓\nAuthentication\n ↓\nrequest.user = Rahul\n\nThen the backend does:\ntask = Task.objects.create(\n    title=request.data[\"title\"],\n    project=project,\n    created_by=request.user\n)\n\nThe client doesn't send:\n{\n  \"created_by\": 99\n}\n\nImagine Rahul tries:\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"Delete database\",\n  \"created_by\": 1\n}\n\nIf we trust the request body, Rahul could pretend that Amit created the task.\nThat's a security and data-integrity problem.\n\ncreated_by should be populated by the backend using the authenticated user from request.user. \nWe should never trust the client to provide the creator's identity because the client can modify \nrequest data. The authenticated identity comes from the verified authentication mechanism.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "B) Backend gets the authenticated user from request.user.\n\nWhy?\ncreated_by represents who actually performed the action.\nThe client should not be allowed to decide that.\nFor example, Rahul sends:\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"Fix login bug\"\n}\n\nThe request contains Rahul's JWT:\n\nJWT\n ↓\nuser_id = 42\n ↓\nAuthentication\n ↓\nrequest.user = Rahul\n\nThen the backend does:\ntask = Task.objects.create(\n    title=request.data[\"title\"],\n    project=project,\n    created_by=request.user\n)\n\nThe client doesn't send:\n{\n  \"created_by\": 99\n}\n\nImagine Rahul tries:\n\n{\n  \"title\": \"Delete database\",\n  \"created_by\": 1\n}\n\nIf we trust the request body, Rahul could pretend that Amit created the task.\nThat's a security and data-integrity problem.\n\ncreated_by should be populated by the backend using the authenticated user from request.user. \nWe should never trust the client to provide the creator's identity because the client can modify \nrequest data. The authenticated identity comes from the verified authentication mechanism.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "FILESalElC5Ncq06UnOQt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 272.4619465977696,
      "y": 32166.783171524046,
      "width": 1067,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b52",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 839290462,
      "version": 24,
      "versionNonce": 1489868521,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789964466120,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 11 — assigned_to\nNow let's test an important authorization rule.\nRahul is working in:\nGoogle\n  └── Engineering Team\n\nRahul creates a task in Engineering.\nHe sends:\n\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix API\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 999\n}\n\nBut user 999 belongs to TechNova, not Google.\n\nShould the backend allow this assignment?\n\nA) Yes, because assigned_to is just a user ID\nB) No, because the assigned user must have appropriate membership/access to the organization/team\nC) Yes, if user 999 exists\nD) It depends only on the frontend\n\nWhat's your answer and why?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 11 — assigned_to\nNow let's test an important authorization rule.\nRahul is working in:\nGoogle\n  └── Engineering Team\n\nRahul creates a task in Engineering.\nHe sends:\n\n{\n    \"title\": \"Fix API\",\n    \"assigned_to\": 999\n}\n\nBut user 999 belongs to TechNova, not Google.\n\nShould the backend allow this assignment?\n\nA) Yes, because assigned_to is just a user ID\nB) No, because the assigned user must have appropriate membership/access to the organization/team\nC) Yes, if user 999 exists\nD) It depends only on the frontend\n\nWhat's your answer and why?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "exEuV-5JwdwmQCQOTEXq7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 315.2119465977696,
      "y": 32809.537077774046,
      "width": 1078,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b53",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 195017986,
      "version": 30,
      "versionNonce": 363489319,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789964525638,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "B) No, because the assigned user must have appropriate membership/access to the organization/team.\nThis is an important backend security rule.\n\nWhy?\n\nA user ID existing in the database doesn't mean that user has access to this resource.\n\nThe backend should verify something like:\n\nTask\n ↓\nProject\n ↓\nTeam\n ↓\nOrganization\n ↓\nIs assigned_to a valid member?\nThe backend must validate that the user being assigned to a task has access to the relevant \norganization/team/project. We should never rely only on the existence of the user ID, because \nthat could allow cross-tenant or unauthorized access.\n\n🔥 Important concept: Tenant Isolation\n\nWhat we just discussed is called tenant isolation.\nOur organizations are tenants:\n\nGoogle\n ├── Users\n ├── Teams\n ├── Projects\n └── Tasks\n\nTechNova\n ├── Users\n ├── Teams\n ├── Projects\n └── Tasks\nA Google user should not accidentally be able to access or modify TechNova's data.\nThis is one of the most important concepts in our multi-tenant SaaS architecture.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "B) No, because the assigned user must have appropriate membership/access to the organization/team.\nThis is an important backend security rule.\n\nWhy?\n\nA user ID existing in the database doesn't mean that user has access to this resource.\n\nThe backend should verify something like:\n\nTask\n ↓\nProject\n ↓\nTeam\n ↓\nOrganization\n ↓\nIs assigned_to a valid member?\nThe backend must validate that the user being assigned to a task has access to the relevant \norganization/team/project. We should never rely only on the existence of the user ID, because \nthat could allow cross-tenant or unauthorized access.\n\n🔥 Important concept: Tenant Isolation\n\nWhat we just discussed is called tenant isolation.\nOur organizations are tenants:\n\nGoogle\n ├── Users\n ├── Teams\n ├── Projects\n └── Tasks\n\nTechNova\n ├── Users\n ├── Teams\n ├── Projects\n └── Tasks\nA Google user should not accidentally be able to access or modify TechNova's data.\nThis is one of the most important concepts in our multi-tenant SaaS architecture.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-l5wiNTvArU0gipPhXv7d",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 239.4619465977696,
      "y": 33920.665984024046,
      "width": 352,
      "height": 375,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b54",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1433344030,
      "version": 15,
      "versionNonce": 1051513289,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789964656228,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Question 12\n\nNow imagine Rahul sends:\nGET /api/projects/500/tasks/\nProject 500 belongs to TechNova.\n\nRahul belongs only to Google.\nWhat should the API return?\n\nA) All tasks from Project 500\nB) 403 Forbidden\nC) 404 Not Found\nD) Empty list []\n\nAnd why?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Question 12\n\nNow imagine Rahul sends:\nGET /api/projects/500/tasks/\nProject 500 belongs to TechNova.\n\nRahul belongs only to Google.\nWhat should the API return?\n\nA) All tasks from Project 500\nB) 403 Forbidden\nC) 404 Not Found\nD) Empty list []\n\nAnd why?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Wo9W37zTQJGsi9vajiFmt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 291.5869465977696,
      "y": 34341.790984024046,
      "width": 1100,
      "height": 1425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b55",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 504464258,
      "version": 60,
      "versionNonce": 1821952839,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789965106783,
      "created": 1789964800189,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "401 vs 403\nThis is important for interviews:\n\n401 Unauthorized\n→ We don't know/authenticate the user.\n\n403 Forbidden\n→ We know who the user is, but they don't have permission.\n\nRahul has a valid JWT, so this is not 401.\n\nBut why did I give you a subtle question?\nSometimes returning 403 reveals that the resource exists.\n\nFor example: GET /projects/500\nResponse: 403 Forbidden\n\nRahul can infer:\n\"Project 500 exists, but I don't have access.\"\n\nIn a multi-tenant system, that can leak information about another organization's resources.\nTherefore, depending on the API/security design, we may deliberately return:\n404 Not Found\n\nfor unauthorized access, making it appear as though the resource doesn't exist.\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nA user who is authenticated but lacks permission would conceptually receive 403 Forbidden. \nHowever, for multi-tenant resources, returning 404 Not Found can be preferable when we don't \nwant to reveal whether a resource belonging to another tenant exists. The choice depends on \nthe API's security requirements.\n\nSo your answer B is conceptually correct, but the stronger interview answer is:\n\n403 for authorization failure; 404 can be intentionally used to prevent resource-existence \nleakage.\n\nLet's stop the authorization section here.\nYou've now covered some very important backend concepts:\n\nAuthentication\n     ↓\nWho is the user?\n     ↓\nAuthorization\n     ↓\nWhat can the user access?\n     ↓\nTenant isolation\n     ↓\nOrganization → Team → Project → Task\n     ↓\nRole / Permission\n\nNext, we'll move into Django REST Framework API structure — serializers, views, validation, service \nlayer, and how an actual request flows through our project.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "401 vs 403\nThis is important for interviews:\n\n401 Unauthorized\n→ We don't know/authenticate the user.\n\n403 Forbidden\n→ We know who the user is, but they don't have permission.\n\nRahul has a valid JWT, so this is not 401.\n\nBut why did I give you a subtle question?\nSometimes returning 403 reveals that the resource exists.\n\nFor example: GET /projects/500\nResponse: 403 Forbidden\n\nRahul can infer:\n\"Project 500 exists, but I don't have access.\"\n\nIn a multi-tenant system, that can leak information about another organization's resources.\nTherefore, depending on the API/security design, we may deliberately return:\n404 Not Found\n\nfor unauthorized access, making it appear as though the resource doesn't exist.\n\n🎯 Interview-quality answer\n\nA user who is authenticated but lacks permission would conceptually receive 403 Forbidden. \nHowever, for multi-tenant resources, returning 404 Not Found can be preferable when we don't \nwant to reveal whether a resource belonging to another tenant exists. The choice depends on \nthe API's security requirements.\n\nSo your answer B is conceptually correct, but the stronger interview answer is:\n\n403 for authorization failure; 404 can be intentionally used to prevent resource-existence \nleakage.\n\nLet's stop the authorization section here.\nYou've now covered some very important backend concepts:\n\nAuthentication\n     ↓\nWho is the user?\n     ↓\nAuthorization\n     ↓\nWhat can the user access?\n     ↓\nTenant isolation\n     ↓\nOrganization → Team → Project → Task\n     ↓\nRole / Permission\n\nNext, we'll move into Django REST Framework API structure — serializers, views, validation, service \nlayer, and how an actual request flows through our project.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
